WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.620
You know, usually when we talk about a medical diagnosis, there is this underlying expectation of mechanical precision, I think.

00:07.720 --> 00:09.660
Right, yeah, like we want it to feel like engineering.

00:09.860 --> 00:17.040
Exactly, like you fall off your bike, you break your arm, and the x-ray shows that jagged white line on a black background.

00:17.260 --> 00:20.980
And the doctor just points at it and says, you know, there it is, that's the problem.

00:21.160 --> 00:22.600
It's the comfort of the visible, really.

00:22.600 --> 00:31.300
We have this deep-seated desire for our biology to behave like a machine made of static local parts.

00:31.860 --> 00:36.800
A gear is broken, you find the gear, and then you just replace or fix the gear.

00:37.080 --> 00:44.260
But then, I mean, you step into the world of psychiatric neurodevelopment or, you know, chronic systemic illness or trauma.

00:44.520 --> 00:46.680
And suddenly that x-ray machine is entirely useless.

00:47.020 --> 00:48.040
Completely useless.

00:48.040 --> 00:53.260
We find ourselves looking at a diagnostic landscape that isn't a broken gear at all.

00:53.440 --> 00:55.100
It is just incredibly murky.

00:55.400 --> 00:58.580
It is the absolute definition of diagnostic muddy waters.

00:58.800 --> 00:58.940
Yeah.

00:59.120 --> 01:04.900
When you look at complex systems, that whole broken part paradigm completely falls apart.

01:05.180 --> 01:11.580
I mean, you can have two people with the exact same diagnosis who share almost no physical similarities at the biological level.

01:11.660 --> 01:15.700
And that murkiness is exactly what we were waiting into for this deep dive.

01:15.800 --> 01:16.200
Oh, definitely.

01:16.200 --> 01:20.340
We have a mission today, and it is squarely aimed at you, the listener.

01:20.900 --> 01:26.320
Our goal is to fundamentally dismantle the way you think about biology, health, and disease.

01:26.460 --> 01:26.960
That'd be a goal.

01:27.140 --> 01:27.960
It really is.

01:28.240 --> 01:33.420
Because for centuries, we have treated the body and the brain like mechanical clocks.

01:33.660 --> 01:38.580
But today, we're looking at a stack of sources that suggest biology isn't a machine at all.

01:38.640 --> 01:38.900
Right.

01:38.980 --> 01:41.520
It's a landscape of dynamic, invisible forces.

01:41.520 --> 01:44.740
What our primary source calls constraint fields.

01:44.740 --> 01:45.980
Constraint fields.

01:46.300 --> 01:51.980
Which, I mean, that phrase alone sounds like we are drifting into quantum physics or astrophysics, right?

01:51.980 --> 01:52.480
Yeah, it does.

01:52.800 --> 01:56.700
But we are talking about squishy, macroscopic biology here.

01:57.020 --> 02:01.300
We are talking about human tissue, human brains, and human behavior.

02:01.300 --> 02:07.200
So we have this really dense, incredibly fascinating theoretical paper from May 2026.

02:07.320 --> 02:07.840
Really fascinating.

02:07.900 --> 02:16.760
It's by an independent researcher named Fliction, titled, Toward a Field Theoretic Interpretation of Neural Heterogeneity and Collective Cell Migration.

02:16.940 --> 02:18.440
A very academic title.

02:18.520 --> 02:19.580
Yeah, quite a mouthful.

02:19.580 --> 02:23.940
Now, Fliction didn't, like, run a new lab experiment here.

02:24.220 --> 02:27.040
Instead, they did something potentially much more profound.

02:27.280 --> 02:27.540
Mm-hmm.

02:27.620 --> 02:34.340
They took three highly specific, newly published empirical studies from completely different fields of biology.

02:34.540 --> 02:34.800
Right.

02:35.140 --> 02:38.700
And they connected them with a single underlying mathematical framework.

02:38.700 --> 02:43.420
And the superficial disconnect between these three studies is what makes this so compelling.

02:43.640 --> 02:43.940
Exactly.

02:44.120 --> 02:50.780
First, we have a large-scale neuroimaging study on generalized anxiety disorder and how it affects the physical aging of the brain.

02:50.900 --> 02:52.300
Okay, so an anxious brain.

02:52.420 --> 02:52.560
Right.

02:53.080 --> 02:59.600
Second, a microscopic cellular study on how epithelial skin cells migrate collectively to heal a wound.

03:00.080 --> 03:01.040
Crawling skin cells.

03:01.160 --> 03:01.500
Got it.

03:01.500 --> 03:08.840
And third, a highly abstract neurocomputational study on mapping the whole human brain using high-dimensional geometry.

03:09.320 --> 03:13.580
So an anxious brain, crawling skin cells, and mathematical brain mapping.

03:13.860 --> 03:18.480
On the surface, I mean, that is a completely random assortment of scientific literature.

03:18.660 --> 03:19.260
It really is.

03:19.260 --> 03:33.620
But the central mystery that Fliction identifies is why these three things, highly anxious brains, migrating skin cells, and mathematical logic, why do they all share the exact same underlying geometry?

03:33.760 --> 03:34.120
Wow.

03:34.460 --> 03:34.660
Yeah.

03:34.960 --> 03:46.340
When you strip away the messy biological details, you know, the chemicals, the tissue types, and just look at the organizational dynamics, the rules governing what these systems are allowed to do are identical.

03:46.340 --> 03:51.580
Okay, let's unpack this, because we really need to ground this in the real world before we get lost in the math.

03:51.840 --> 03:52.180
Good idea.

03:52.400 --> 03:54.420
Let's look at the first real-world anomaly.

03:54.600 --> 03:59.640
Affliction highlights how generalized anxiety disorder actually affects brain aging.

04:00.120 --> 04:03.360
We're talking about the 2026 study by Richier and colleagues.

04:03.500 --> 04:03.660
Right.

04:03.780 --> 04:07.060
This was a massive undertaking within the Enigma Anxiety Consortium.

04:07.080 --> 04:08.620
Which is a huge group, right?

04:08.700 --> 04:08.840
Yeah.

04:08.920 --> 04:13.040
Enigma is this huge collaborative network that pools brain scans from all over the world.

04:13.040 --> 04:16.540
They do that to get sample sizes large enough to actually see subtle effects.

04:17.160 --> 04:25.920
So, Richier's team was looking specifically at structural brain aging in people diagnosed with generalized anxiety disorder, or GAD.

04:26.200 --> 04:26.620
Exactly.

04:26.880 --> 04:29.740
Let's define what we mean by structural aging here, just to be clear.

04:29.900 --> 04:34.040
We aren't just talking about a person feeling older or complaining of memory loss, right?

04:34.360 --> 04:35.440
No, not at all.

04:35.660 --> 04:39.520
We are talking about the actual physical morphology of the brain.

04:39.600 --> 04:41.060
Okay, that's an important distinction.

04:41.060 --> 04:45.800
Yeah, as we age normally, our brains undergo predictable physical changes.

04:46.240 --> 04:50.340
The cortex, you know, the outer layer of gray matter, it tends to thin out.

04:50.460 --> 04:50.700
Right.

04:50.880 --> 04:53.880
The fluid-filled ventricles in the center of the brain expand.

04:54.240 --> 04:57.460
The folds of the brain, the sulci, they tend to whiten.

04:57.720 --> 04:59.220
So they can see this on a scan.

04:59.400 --> 04:59.600
Right.

05:00.060 --> 05:07.720
And Richier's team used a convolutional neural network, a CNN, to analyze thousands of these structural MRI scans.

05:07.720 --> 05:13.780
I want to pause on the CNN aspect, because machine learning in medicine can sometimes feel like a bit of a black box.

05:13.940 --> 05:14.380
Oh, completely.

05:14.660 --> 05:18.880
How is a convolutional neural network actually determining someone's age from an MRI?

05:19.280 --> 05:22.040
Is it like just looking for gray hair on the inside of your skull?

05:22.280 --> 05:23.400
No, no gray hair.

05:23.800 --> 05:29.080
It's essentially performing a highly complex pattern recognition task across a 3D landscape.

05:29.080 --> 05:38.100
A CNN works by passing filters over the image, looking for features, edges, curves, gradients of tissue density, that sort of thing.

05:38.200 --> 05:39.900
So it's looking for shapes, basically.

05:40.380 --> 05:40.960
Sort of.

05:41.440 --> 05:45.360
In the early layers, it might just notice the boundary between gray matter and white matter.

05:45.440 --> 05:45.600
Yeah.

05:45.960 --> 05:52.880
But in the deeper layers, it learns to associate complex, whole-brain geometric patterns with chronological age.

05:52.980 --> 05:53.700
Oh, I see.

05:53.700 --> 05:58.880
You train it on thousands of healthy brains, telling it, this scan is a 20-year-old, this scan is a 60-year-old.

05:59.420 --> 06:03.960
Eventually, the network learns the mathematical signature of a healthy 40-year-old brain.

06:04.220 --> 06:07.200
So once a CNN is trained, you can just hand it a new brain scan.

06:07.340 --> 06:10.100
You don't tell it the person's real age and see what the computer guesses.

06:10.360 --> 06:10.660
Yes.

06:11.120 --> 06:15.060
And the core metric they derive from this is called the predicted age difference, or PA.

06:15.580 --> 06:16.780
Predicted age difference.

06:16.920 --> 06:17.060
Right.

06:17.060 --> 06:24.100
You take the age the CNN predicts based purely on the physical structure, and you subtract the person's actual chronological age.

06:24.400 --> 06:30.600
Meaning, if I am 30 years old, but the CNN looks at the physical wear and tear on my cortex and says,

06:31.020 --> 06:35.900
uh, this looks like a 40-year-old brain, my pad is positive 10.

06:36.020 --> 06:36.420
Exactly.

06:36.680 --> 06:38.880
Your brain looks 10 years older than you actually are.

06:38.940 --> 06:39.160
Right.

06:39.380 --> 06:44.220
And conversely, if the CNN predicts your brain looks 25, your PD is negative 5.

06:44.300 --> 06:44.660
Got it.

06:44.660 --> 06:54.580
A negative PD generally means the brain looks younger, or at least it exhibits a structural pattern that is atypical in a, like, a delayed or prolonged developmental sense.

06:54.740 --> 06:58.120
Which brings us to the core hypothesis of the Enigma study.

06:58.680 --> 07:07.940
Based on our traditional machine view of the human body, the logical assumption is that a chronic pathology like generalized anxiety disorder acts as an accelerant.

07:08.060 --> 07:08.280
Right.

07:08.280 --> 07:14.580
You think about the chronic stress, the elevated cortisol levels bathing the brain, the constant hypervigilance and threat anticipation.

07:14.780 --> 07:15.520
Yeah, it's exhausting.

07:15.620 --> 07:19.660
It stands to reason that this engine is running too hot, and it's going to wear the parts down faster.

07:19.780 --> 07:21.780
And the researchers hypothesized exactly that.

07:21.780 --> 07:27.020
They expected the GAD patients as a group to have a significantly higher mean PD.

07:27.280 --> 07:28.180
A higher average.

07:28.500 --> 07:28.800
Right.

07:29.320 --> 07:41.160
They assumed the stress would systematically force these brains to age prematurely, shifting the entire bell curve of the GAD population to the right toward an older structural phenotype.

07:41.440 --> 07:44.680
But the results completely falsified this hypothesis, didn't they?

07:44.680 --> 07:45.120
Completely.

07:45.780 --> 07:54.980
When they looked at the population average, the mean PD for people with generalized anxiety disorder wasn't really elevated at all compared to the healthy control group.

07:55.060 --> 07:56.320
So it was just normal.

07:56.460 --> 08:03.240
Well, if you were only looking at the group average, the mean GAD is basically invisible on a structural MRI.

08:03.540 --> 08:04.020
Wow.

08:04.020 --> 08:07.620
Under normal scientific circumstances, this would be a failed study.

08:07.620 --> 08:16.060
The researchers would write a paper saying, we found no significant effect of GAD on brain aging, and the scientific community would just move on.

08:16.400 --> 08:21.740
But they didn't do that because they noticed something in the data that was much more subtle and much more profound.

08:22.000 --> 08:22.400
Exactly.

08:22.480 --> 08:24.980
While the mean didn't shift, the variance exploded.

08:25.280 --> 08:26.380
The variance was huge.

08:26.860 --> 08:31.560
The data points for the GAD population were massively spread out compared to the healthy controls.

08:31.940 --> 08:35.900
Yeah, and this was particularly dramatic in adults over the age of 25.

08:35.900 --> 08:42.520
Which is typically when the human brain finishes its major developmental consolidation, right?

08:42.600 --> 08:43.040
Exactly.

08:43.660 --> 08:49.820
In a healthy population, the predicted ages cluster fairly tightly around their actual chronological ages.

08:50.440 --> 08:51.480
The variance is small.

08:51.840 --> 08:55.900
But in the GAD population, that tight cluster was just blown apart.

08:56.120 --> 08:59.880
This completely upends how we think about a psychiatric diagnosis.

09:00.080 --> 09:00.760
It really does.

09:00.760 --> 09:08.380
I mean, we have patients with the exact same clinical label generalized anxiety disorder who are suffering from the same subjective symptoms of worry and distress.

09:08.800 --> 09:08.880
Right.

09:08.880 --> 09:15.200
But biologically, some of these patients have brains that look vastly older, heavily consolidated, and rigid.

09:15.540 --> 09:23.940
And others with the exact same diagnosis have brains that look vastly younger, stuck in this prolonged, diffuse, highly plastic state.

09:24.180 --> 09:29.980
It tells us that the diagnostic category of GAD does not map to a single point in developmental space.

09:30.040 --> 09:30.140
Right.

09:30.200 --> 09:31.680
It's not a specific broken gear.

09:31.680 --> 09:35.280
No, it is a widened cloud of possibilities.

09:36.440 --> 09:40.960
The disorder does not force a single biological destiny onto the patient.

09:41.660 --> 09:45.780
Instead, it amplifies the sensitivity of your developmental trajectory.

09:46.000 --> 09:47.260
I'm trying to visualize this.

09:47.320 --> 09:51.520
It's like assuming anxiety is a sniper rifle aiming directly at premature aging.

09:51.680 --> 09:52.280
Okay, yeah.

09:52.340 --> 09:54.760
But the data shows it's actually a shotgun blast.

09:54.880 --> 09:56.940
It just scatters the trajectory entirely.

09:57.180 --> 09:58.280
That's a great way to put it.

09:58.280 --> 10:04.100
The underlying stress of the condition takes the normal path of brain development and makes it highly unstable.

10:04.640 --> 10:10.060
Some brains lock down and consolidate prematurely as a defense mechanism against the stress.

10:10.520 --> 10:15.520
Others fail to consolidate at all and stay in this labile, overly open state.

10:15.760 --> 10:17.900
The clinical implications of this are staggering.

10:18.120 --> 10:18.240
Yeah.

10:18.320 --> 10:23.240
I mean, if you try to treat the average GAD patient, you are treating a mathematical ghost.

10:23.440 --> 10:23.880
A ghost, yeah.

10:23.920 --> 10:26.380
You are treating a mean that doesn't actually exist in reality.

10:26.660 --> 10:27.980
So what does this all mean?

10:27.980 --> 10:42.700
If GAD doesn't have a single neurological signature, if our entire way of diagnosing and categorizing mental health based on population averages is missing the point, are we just using the wrong statistical tools?

10:43.100 --> 10:44.860
We are definitely using the wrong tools.

10:45.380 --> 10:50.540
Infliction addresses this directly by pointing out what they call the insufficiency of univariate reduction.

10:50.620 --> 10:52.000
Okay, univariate reduction.

10:52.000 --> 10:52.280
Right.

10:52.640 --> 10:57.080
For decades, traditional neuroimaging has relied heavily on univariate analysis.

10:57.880 --> 11:04.280
You take a 3D brain scan and you break it down into thousands of tiny isolated 3D pixels called voxels.

11:04.800 --> 11:08.760
Like breaking a high resolution photograph down into individual colored square.

11:08.920 --> 11:09.280
Exactly.

11:09.560 --> 11:17.140
And then you measure the volume or the cortical thickness in each isolated voxel independently and you average that single voxel across the entire population.

11:17.140 --> 11:17.820
Oh, I see.

11:17.960 --> 11:24.120
You are essentially asking, is voxel number 4002 systematically thinner in anxious people than in healthy people?

11:24.200 --> 11:25.580
So you are looking for the Birkin pixel.

11:25.920 --> 11:29.420
You want the x-ray to show the jagged line in one specific spot.

11:29.640 --> 11:29.960
Yes.

11:30.460 --> 11:35.500
But by doing that, you completely destroy the distributed relational structure of the brain.

11:35.580 --> 11:35.680
Yeah.

11:35.800 --> 11:37.180
You lose the forest for the trees.

11:37.300 --> 11:37.440
Right.

11:37.440 --> 11:44.500
The CNN in the Richer study caught the massive variance in GAD precisely because it doesn't look at isolated pixels.

11:44.800 --> 11:47.600
It maps the high-dimensional brain configuration manifold.

11:48.120 --> 11:51.380
Okay, let's define manifold here because that's a term that's going to come up again.

11:51.460 --> 11:51.620
Sure.

11:51.880 --> 11:55.940
A manifold is a topological space that locally resembles Euclidean space.

11:56.120 --> 11:56.640
Right.

11:56.780 --> 11:56.960
Okay.

11:57.100 --> 11:57.760
Simpler terms.

11:58.080 --> 12:01.220
For our purposes, think of it as a landscape of relationships.

12:01.220 --> 12:09.440
The CNN looks at how voxel A relates to voxel B and how both relate to the curvature of voxel C.

12:09.720 --> 12:10.000
Got it.

12:10.140 --> 12:13.040
It evaluates the entire structural geometry at once.

12:13.260 --> 12:18.680
And what the CNN revealed is that the pathology of GAD isn't localized to a broken part.

12:18.780 --> 12:19.000
Right.

12:19.140 --> 12:23.900
The pathology is that the possibility space of the brain's development have been blown wide open.

12:23.900 --> 12:28.220
This concept of trajectory widening is staggering to me.

12:28.220 --> 12:34.900
To think that a mental health disorder isn't a destination, but rather a warping of your developmental elasticity.

12:35.320 --> 12:35.520
Exactly.

12:35.700 --> 12:37.200
You aren't destined for one outcome.

12:37.440 --> 12:37.620
Right.

12:37.720 --> 12:39.860
The rails guiding your development have just been removed.

12:40.080 --> 12:45.100
But this is where Fliction's paper makes a massive, almost unbelievable leap.

12:45.520 --> 12:51.580
Because it's one thing to say that a psychological disorder widens the trajectory of brain development over a lifespan.

12:51.580 --> 13:03.900
It is another thing entirely to apply this abstract geometry of possibilities to physical, squishy, microscopic biology playing out over a matter of minutes.

13:04.040 --> 13:07.580
Which leads us directly into the second empirical study Fliction incorporates.

13:08.340 --> 13:08.540
Right.

13:08.620 --> 13:09.380
The skin cells.

13:09.480 --> 13:09.700
Yeah.

13:09.760 --> 13:17.460
We are leaving the complex macro-level architecture of the ancient human brain and traveling down to the microscopic level of the migrating skin cell.

13:17.540 --> 13:17.860
Okay.

13:17.860 --> 13:25.920
Fliction turns to a 2026 paper by Hirano and colleagues that looked at a specific protein called ZO1 in epithelial cells.

13:26.160 --> 13:28.800
Now, we know epithelial cells are the barrier cells.

13:29.140 --> 13:30.080
They line our organs.

13:30.200 --> 13:31.120
They form our skin.

13:31.300 --> 13:31.420
Right.

13:31.640 --> 13:35.260
Their primary job, for the most part, is to stay put and maintain a boundary.

13:35.500 --> 13:41.160
And in standard biology textbooks, the protein ZO1 is seen as a static component of that boundary.

13:41.380 --> 13:43.980
It sits at what are called apical junctional complexes.

13:44.400 --> 13:44.680
Okay.

13:44.880 --> 13:46.340
Apical junctional complexes.

13:46.340 --> 13:55.200
If you imagine a brick wall, the epithelial cells of the bricks, and ZO1 is a key part of the mortar, holding those bricks together near the top edge.

13:55.300 --> 13:55.600
Got it.

13:55.660 --> 13:56.100
The mortar.

13:56.480 --> 14:01.560
It maintains the integrity of the barrier so that fluids and pathogens don't leak through.

14:01.700 --> 14:05.280
But the Hirano study wasn't looking at a healthy static barrier.

14:05.640 --> 14:09.020
They were looking at what happens when these cells need to move.

14:09.120 --> 14:09.280
Right.

14:09.280 --> 14:13.480
For instance, when you get a cut on your arm, the skin cells don't just divide.

14:13.720 --> 14:17.400
They actually have to crawl collectively across the wound bed to close the gap.

14:17.400 --> 14:22.380
And this is where the textbook model of ZO1 as static mortar breaks down entirely.

14:22.940 --> 14:27.900
When the tissue needs to migrate, Hirano's team observed a wave of biochemical activation.

14:28.620 --> 14:33.540
Specifically, the activation of a protein called ERK sweeping across the tissue.

14:33.540 --> 14:35.620
I want to challenge this idea of a wave.

14:36.020 --> 14:39.680
Because usually when we think of chemicals in biology, we think of diffusion.

14:40.120 --> 14:40.220
Right.

14:40.540 --> 14:43.840
A drop of ink in a glass of water and it spreads out passively.

14:44.220 --> 14:47.260
Or a cell releases a signal and it drifts outward.

14:47.680 --> 14:49.180
Why are we calling this a wave?

14:49.440 --> 14:50.660
That's a crucial distinction.

14:51.380 --> 14:52.720
It is not passive diffusion.

14:53.720 --> 14:56.240
The ERK activation does not just happen randomly.

14:56.240 --> 14:59.180
And it doesn't fade out smoothly like ink in water.

14:59.520 --> 15:00.180
Oh, really?

15:00.480 --> 15:00.740
No.

15:01.040 --> 15:03.260
It propagates as a literal traveling wave.

15:03.560 --> 15:11.700
A coordinated front of biochemical activity that sweeps across the sheet of cells, maintaining its shape and intensity as it moves.

15:11.920 --> 15:12.440
Wow.

15:12.900 --> 15:20.640
And as this highly coordinated wave washes over a specific epithelial cell, the ZO1 protein does something wild, doesn't it?

15:20.720 --> 15:21.460
It really does.

15:21.540 --> 15:24.580
It physically detaches from the tight junctions at the top of the cell.

15:24.580 --> 15:27.020
The mortar literally leaves the brick wall.

15:27.340 --> 15:27.980
That's crazy.

15:28.220 --> 15:32.020
And it moves down to the base of the cell, interacting with these structures called potosomes.

15:32.300 --> 15:32.740
Potosomes.

15:32.860 --> 15:33.700
Let's talk about those.

15:33.980 --> 15:35.600
Cotosomes are fascinating structures.

15:35.740 --> 15:37.620
You can think of them as little invasive feet.

15:38.280 --> 15:44.920
When a cell needs to crawl, it extends these potosomes downward into the extracellular matrix, the scaffolding beneath the cells.

15:45.020 --> 15:45.260
Okay.

15:45.760 --> 15:49.760
The potosomes help the cell grip the matrix, pull itself forward, and crawl.

15:49.760 --> 15:56.280
So the glue holding the top of the wall together suddenly drops down to the bottom and becomes the feet helping the wall walk.

15:56.720 --> 15:59.680
What is happening here is a true phase transition.

16:00.640 --> 16:07.760
The cell switches entirely from a junction maintenance regime into a potosome invasive regime.

16:07.940 --> 16:08.080
Right.

16:08.080 --> 16:13.020
The ERK wave forces a transient reallocation of constraint density.

16:13.420 --> 16:15.180
Reallocation of constraint density.

16:15.340 --> 16:16.860
Let's translate that for everyone.

16:17.200 --> 16:21.620
It means the rules governing the cell's physical structure are temporarily rewritten.

16:22.420 --> 16:30.220
For a brief window of time, the cell's internal constraints shift from holding on to its neighbors to pulling itself forward across the substrate.

16:30.420 --> 16:32.320
It's like a wave going around a sports stadium.

16:32.480 --> 16:33.180
Oh, I like that.

16:33.180 --> 16:39.780
Yeah, when the wave hits your specific section, you stand up, throw your arms in the air, and totally change your structural role for a few seconds.

16:39.980 --> 16:40.460
Exactly.

16:40.600 --> 16:47.080
You break the static shape of the seated crowd, but then you sit back down to maintain the crowd's structure.

16:47.260 --> 16:51.280
Because crucially, this Z01 shift is completely reversible.

16:51.640 --> 16:52.680
It has to be reversible.

16:52.880 --> 16:59.600
I mean, if it were irreversible, if the mortar stayed gone permanently, the migrating tissue would lose all its structural integrity.

16:59.640 --> 17:00.480
It would just fall apart.

17:00.480 --> 17:07.260
It would completely disintegrate into a chaotic soup of disconnected cells, and the wound would never heal properly.

17:07.380 --> 17:07.700
Right.

17:08.060 --> 17:15.200
Once the ERK wave passes, the Z01 protein physically relocates right back to the apical tight junctions.

17:15.860 --> 17:18.120
The cohesion of the brick wall is restored.

17:18.400 --> 17:18.860
Wow.

17:19.340 --> 17:26.500
The tissue behaves as a coupled dynamical medium, where the wave coordinates the movement smoothly across both space and time.

17:26.500 --> 17:30.280
I'm still stuck on the difference between diffusion and this traveling wave, though.

17:30.520 --> 17:33.020
